    {
     "id": "wyxp27wx",
     "type": "text",
     "x": 470,
     "y": 720,
     "text": "Double-click any block to open its editor. Select a 3D figure to turn it or change it in the panel.",
     "tw": 0,
     "size": "small",
     "font": "default",
     "bold": false,
     "italic": false,
     "color": "#1e63c4",
     "opacity": 1
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "# How to use\n## Draw\n1. Pick a tool from the rail on the left. A button with a small corner mark opens a menu when you rest on it: lines, shapes, electrical, physics, \\textbf{2D plots}, \\textbf{3D} and \\textbf{Blocks}.\n2. Click on the canvas to place the object, or drag to set its size. Hold \\texttt{Shift} to keep angles and squares, \\texttt{Alt} to place it off the grid.\n3. Choose the arrow tool (\\texttt{V}) to select. The panel on the right edits what is selected: colours, line style, arrow heads, text size and exact position.\n- Shortcuts for the common tools: \\texttt{L} line, \\texttt{A} arrow, \\texttt{C} curve, \\texttt{P} polyline, \\texttt{R} rectangle, \\texttt{E} ellipse, \\texttt{T} text.\n\n## Text and maths\n- Write maths between \\$ signs, as in \\LaTeX, for example $\\int_0^1 x^2\\,dx = \\tfrac13$. Use \\$\\$ for a displayed formula.\n- Double-click any text or label to edit it in place. Under \\textbf{Text input} in the sidebar, choose \\LaTeX\\ source or the visual MathLive editor.\n\n## Blocks\n- Circuits, quantum circuits, commutative diagrams, trees, ZX and Feynman diagrams, Bloch spheres, 3D geometry, timing diagrams, state machines, Karnaugh maps and chemistry are each drawn from their own \\LaTeX\\ package.\n- Double-click a block, or select it and press \\texttt{Enter}, to open its editor. The drawing and the code beside it stay in step, both ways.\n\n## Arrange\n- Right-click an object for its actions. \\texttt{Cmd+D} duplicates, \\texttt{Cmd+G} groups, \\texttt{Cmd+R} turns by 90 degrees, and the arrow keys nudge.\n- Hold \\texttt{Space} and drag to pan, or \\texttt{Cmd} and scroll to zoom. \\texttt{G} shows or hides the grid. \\texttt{Cmd+Z} undoes.\n\n## Write the document\n- In Classic, switch between \\textbf{Document} and \\textbf{Diagram} at the top, or double-click a diagram to draw in it.\n- In a text block, start a line with \\texttt{\\#} for a section, \\texttt{-} for a bullet or \\texttt{1.} for a numbered list.\n- Your work saves in this browser as you go. \\texttt{Cmd+S} saves a \\texttt{.diagramtex} file to keep or share, and \\texttt{Cmd+O} opens one. \\textbf{Import} reads TikZ or \\LaTeX\\ back in.\n\n## Get the LaTeX out\n- In Diagram view, \\textbf{Code} (\\texttt{Cmd+E}) shows the TikZ for the diagram, ready to paste into your paper. It lists the packages your preamble needs.\n- \\textbf{LaTeX} (\\texttt{Cmd+Shift+E}) downloads the whole document as a \\texttt{.tex} file.\n- \\textbf{Export} saves a PNG, SVG or PDF. \\texttt{Cmd+P} prints."
  }
 ]
}